{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And your name shall no longer be called Avram.</b> One who calls Avraham 'Avram' transgresses a positive commandment, because [the name] was joined with him in uncircumcision. And one should not refute [this] from 'אשר בחרת באברם' (whom You chose), for there the arranger is praising Avraham by citing what his name was originally — and the meaning of 'אשר בחרת' is: when his name was Avram; and 'ושמת שמו אברהם' — and You set his name Avraham. But Yaakov reverts and is called Yaakov, because his name was joined with him in Jewishness. And because human beings gave names to these two [Avraham and Yaakov], their names were changed; but Yitzhak, whom HaShem named, as it is written 'וקראת את שמו יצחק' — his name was not changed." "<b>And your name shall be Avraham.</b> It is customary to change a person's name when he rises to greatness — and likewise with Sarah, with Yaakov, with Yitzhak, with Yehoshua, with Hananiah, Mishael, and Azariah. Avram — Avraham: אביר המון גוים (father of a multitude of nations)."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:04:30.775555763Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}